    {
      "id": "canon-templates-in-order-repo-design",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Repo template: the design (proposed)",
      "position": {
        "x": -60,
        "y": 4300
      },
      "width": 1040,
      "height": 1700,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "key": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 270
        }
      ],
      "handles": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "key": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 270,
          "portId": "root"
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "canon-templates-in-order",
          "nodeId": "canon-templates-in-order-repo-design"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "> **Amended.** A shelf is a collection of graphs, and the repo home is a collection of shelves, so the Shelf class below is a **Collection** (its own template, designed first). The Repo is a Collection plus where it lives. The `holds` edge becomes a general one between a collection and its members. The layout rules and the audit stand as approved; the Census does not (membership is a portal and an edge, see the Collection design), so rule 6 below reads as amended.\n\n# Repo template: the design\n\n**Proposed.** Nothing here is built, and nothing is in canon, until you approve it. It meets the twelve-rule standard; where a rule needs a decision from you, it is listed at the bottom.\n\n## What a repo is, here\n\nA repo is a **library**: a home graph and shelves of graphs, kept in a `public/` folder of a GitHub repository. Where a library can be read is a separate matter, and the two are not the same place. A personal library is not on the site at all: the app opens it straight from GitHub (`github://owner/repo/...`), so it is available only through the app, and as soon as it is pushed. Canon is also kept in a GitHub repository, but it is read from your site (`tlz://twilite.zone/...`, an app on your own host), and a graph is visible there only after you publish it. So the template treats the host as a field of the library, not as part of what a repo is. Both have the same shape. Today yours has 21 folders and 500+ graphs, and canon has 18 and nearly 700, and neither follows a written layout. Some graphs sit loose (`handshaketest.node`, 75 files in `graphs/`), some folders have no root graph (`4-27-2026`, `secret`, `workspaces`), and the home graph still carries the old model's Title, Type, Section and Summary nodes and four import bridges.\n\n## 1. What the template contains\n\nOne graph, `library/templates/repo-template/root.node`, built the way the Task Template is:\n\n- **A Repo node** (class *Repo*). Fields: owner, repo, branch, where it is read from (GitHub, or a site, with whether it is published), what the library is for, who it is for, and the home graph. It replaces canon's first-pass `github-repo` class and the old gallery `repo-home-template`.\n- **Shelf nodes** (class *Shelf*). A shelf is a folder of graphs of one kind: its name, what goes on it, and a portal to its own root graph. The starter has two placeholder shelves, so each state is shown (one with graphs, one empty).\n- **A Start here node**, which is a portal to the first graph a visitor should open.\n- **Three looks, derived** by the template's Method: the Detail is the repo card (name, `owner/repo`, branch, purpose, the shelves and how many graphs each holds, Start here); the Summary is the same in miniature; the Glyph is a repo glyph. Nothing is typed twice.\n- **A window in the Detail's shape (3:2)**, no Icon view, a landing page saying what it is, what it derives and what it exports.\n\n## 2. How a library is arranged in a repo\n\nSeven rules. The template carries the first six in its landing page; the seventh is the audit.\n\n1. **The repo root is the home.** `public/root.node` is the Repo graph. It is the only graph at the top.\n2. **A shelf is a folder with a root graph.** `public/<shelf>/root.node` is a Shelf's page; it holds a portal to each graph on it. A folder with no root graph is not a shelf.\n3. **A graph is a folder.** `public/<shelf>/<slug>/root.node`. New graphs have no loose `.node` files; a graph that wants children (a task's attachments, a template's demos) already has a folder. Existing graphs are not broken: when one moves into a folder, every reference to its old address is rewritten in the same step (portals, bridges, `document.url`), and the old file stays as unrendered reference, not deleted. There is no redirect or compatibility layer.\n4. **Shelves are named for what they hold, plural, lower-case** (`tasks`, `people`, `templates`). This is a storage convention only: a shelf may hold different kinds of graph, and the audit notes it rather than failing it. How graphs are grouped to be read is the home's business (rule 6), not the folder's.\n5. **Every graph's identity is its address.** The root's `document.url` and `settings.github` say where the graph lives; the graph id is unique in the library (a copy changes it).\n6. **The home is a collection of shelves, and membership is declared.** The home lists its shelves, and a shelf lists its graphs, as a Collection does: a portal to each member and a `contains` edge from the collection. A graph cannot list a folder, so a **discovery step** (the audit's walker on disk, the app's folder listing when open) reports graphs that are on disk and are not members yet, and adding them is one step. Discovery suggests; it never makes a member, and there is no Census.\n7. **An audit reads the layout.** A script, `scripts/auditLibrary.mjs`, walks a repo folder and reports each rule it breaks, and the same audit runs in the tests over canon. It is read-only; it never moves or deletes anything. Pointed at your library it gives you the real census (what is loose, what has no root, what is stale) as input to the clean-out task.\n\nCanon and a personal library follow the same rules. The only difference is the address scheme.\n\n## 3. What it exports\n\nOne export, with an allowlist: the **Repo** class, the **Shelf** class and the Method that derives the home. A library made from it imports once, as every canon template does. The exported classes carry ports, and the edge between a Repo and its Shelves is a named edge (`holds`, from the repo's *shelves* port to the shelf's *held-by* port), added to the edge classes when this is built.\n\n## What building it involves\n\nNew: the template, the two classes, the `holds` edge class, `scripts/auditLibrary.mjs`, the tests (the template's own, plus `canonTemplates.test.js` picks it up). Changed: canon's `github-repo` class and `repo-home-template` are retired as unrendered reference, not deleted, and `library/templates/root.node` gets the portal. Not touched: your library. The clean-out and converting your home graph are the next task, using the audit's census.\n\n## Decisions for you\n\nYour answers are recorded below, with the three amendments you gave: rule 3 now rewrites references instead of breaking addresses, rule 4 is a storage convention only, and rule 6 has a separate discovery step with a Census. Set the decision to decided if this now says what you mean.\n\n**A. Is a Shelf a class?** Yes. It has identity, properties and relationships.\n\n**B. No loose `.node` files?** Yes, as the layout for new and moved graphs (rule 3), with reference rewriting. Existing addresses are rewritten, never broken, and the old file stays as unrendered reference. The rewrite is part of the clean-out task, using the audit's census.\n\n**C. Two placeholder shelves?** Yes: a *Tasks* shelf with three graphs listed and an empty *Notes* shelf, so both states show. They are placeholders, not canon content.\n\n**D. Does the audit live in the app repo?** Yes, with the tests, beside the conventions it enforces.\n\n**One thing to check.** \"Preserve compatibility with existing addresses\" could mean a redirect layer, which your rule against compatibility layers rules out. I read it as rewriting every reference at the move. If you meant redirects, say so.\n"
        },
        "_placement": {
          "version": 1,
          "state": "manually-shaped",
          "source": "manual",
          "updatedAt": 1791502752029,
          "reason": "node-drag"
        }
      }
    },
