      "data": {
        "title": "Dropping a node on a node",
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "canon-templates-in-order",
          "nodeId": "canon-templates-in-order-task-drop"
        },
        "classRef": "tlz://twilite.zone/library/classes/nodes/task-planning/task.node-class.node",
        "status": "todo",
        "owner": "Michael",
        "body": "There is no event for dropping one node on another on the canvas (only the Elements panel handles a drop). Add one the way the other graph events work: the drop target declares an event, and a Method runs through an invokes edge. First use: dropping a graph on a collection adds it as a member. Design the event, its payload and which nodes may receive a drop, then build it.",
        "_placement": {
          "version": 1,
          "state": "manually-shaped",
          "source": "manual",
          "updatedAt": 1792449096026,
          "reason": "node-drag"
        },
        "startedAt": "2026-10-21T04:04:59.077Z"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "canon-templates-in-order-collection-design",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Collection template: the design (proposed)",
      "position": {
        "x": -60,
        "y": 6200
      },
      "width": 1040,
      "height": 1500,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "key": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 270
        }
      ],
      "handles": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "key": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 270,
          "portId": "root"
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "canon-templates-in-order",
          "nodeId": "canon-templates-in-order-collection-design"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# Collection template: the design\n\n**Proposed.** Nothing is built, and nothing is in canon, until you approve it. The looks below were shown and agreed; this page puts them together with what is not yet decided.\n\nA **collection** is a graph whose job is to show other graphs: a shelf of tasks, the templates in canon, the repo home (a collection of shelves). It replaces the old collection template, the collection window controller and the workboard template.\n\n## 1. The looks\n\n- **Detail.** A page view (one scrolling area, so rule 5 holds). The title and a count, then the members as tiles. The tiles flow by width: as many columns of 110px or wider as fit, never fewer than two. The node starts at the regular 3:2 shape, stretches as rows are added up to a square, then scrolls. An empty collection stays at the regular size and shows a dashed invitation. Because the Detail sets the graph's shape, the Summary and the window follow it, and the Method writes the new height to the window, as the Task Template does.\n- **Summary.** The name, a count and one useful fact (for a collection of tasks, how many are in progress).\n- **Glyph.** A folders mark.\n- **No Icon view.**\n\n## 2. The tile\n\nThe tile is the **portal's own look**, in every place a portal appears, not a special collection card. It reads the member graph live: the member's own Glyph (so a task already shows its state in it), its name, and its two-line description. The member's file is loaded to open the portal anyway, so this costs no extra work and nothing is stored. A member with no description shows its **kind** (Task, Person) in that line, never a blank one. A member that has a state someone has to act on gets a small dot (rule 4); a member that has none shows nothing.\n\nA tile loses what no longer fits, in this order: below about 160px the description, below about 100px the title, then only the glyph. A tile is a link: it opens the member.\n\n## 3. Membership\n\nA **member** is a graph the collection presents. Everything else in the collection graph (notes, labels, the Detail, the Method) is just a node. A graph becomes a member by being **declared**: a Portal node pointing at it, and a `contains` edge from the collection's `members` port to that portal. Removing a member is deleting the edge. So membership is explicit, saved with the graph, and independent of where files sit: a graph can be in several collections, a collection can mix kinds, and moving a file changes only its address (which the clean-out rewrites).\n\nThere is no Census and no stored copy of a member's facts. The tile reads them from the loaded member (section 2). For a collection that is a folder, a **discovery step** (the library audit's walker on disk, the app's folder listing when open) only reports graphs that are on disk and are not members yet, and adding them is one step, or all at once for a shelf. Discovery suggests; it never makes a member. Adding a member by dropping a graph on the collection is the same operation (a portal and an edge), and comes with the drop event, its own task.\n\nOrder is the order of the `contains` edges, with sorting by name or kind as an option. A member does not know which collections show it. Members are graphs only: a node inside a graph is shown by its own class card.\n\n## 4. What it contains and exports\n\nThe graph holds a **Collection** root, the Detail, the Summary and the Glyph, and the Method that lays them out. The Method arranges the member portals in a grid by width, sets the window and Detail height (stretching to a square, then the workspace scrolls it), and writes the count into the Summary. It never looks anything up. It exports the Method and one node class, **Collection**, through a single export with an allowlist. There is no Shelf class: a shelf is a Collection, and the repo template is a Collection plus where it lives.\n\n**One app change comes with it:** a portal is drawn as the tile (this section's look), everywhere. That is part of the Collection task.\n\n**Known limit:** the Summary says how many members there are, not facts about them (such as \"3 in progress\"), because the Method does not read loaded members. If the app later gives a Method read-only access to what a portal has loaded, those aggregates come for free.\n\n## 5. What it is checked by\n\nThe standard's rules (its audit) and tests of its own: the stored views equal what the Method derives; each state (a few members, many members, empty, a member with no description); the tile shrinks as listed; the Detail is a page with exactly one scrolling area.\n\n## Decisions for you\n\n**A. Membership as a portal and a `contains` edge, and the tile as the portal's own look everywhere?** I say yes: nothing is stored, so nothing goes stale. It changes how every portal looks in the app.\n\n**B. The stretch limit and the tile minimum.** A square, and 110px. These are guesses; say if they feel wrong when you see the real one.\n\n**C. Does the old collection template get retired as unrendered reference?** I say yes, as for every other replaced template; nothing is deleted.\n"
        },
        "_placement": {
          "version": 1,
          "state": "manually-shaped",
          "source": "manual",
          "updatedAt": 1791502752029,
          "reason": "node-drag"
        }
      }
    },
